(
    version: 1,
    id: "m06",
    title: "Filter the Noise",
    chapter: 2,
    concepts: ["Noise", "Averaging", "Median"],
    world: (id: "walled_arena", variant: Some("box_ahead")),
    robot: Starter("rover"),
    time_limit_s: 5.0,
    grading_seeds: [1, 2, 3],
    battery_soc: (0.9, 0.9),
    brief_md: r#"
## How far away is the box?

The Rover stands still, looking at a box. Measure the distance from the ultrasonic sensor to the
box and **report it** with `axl_report("distance", metres)`. It must be within 5 mm of the true value
on all three test runs.

One reading is never exactly right. Every reading has a little random noise, so two readings from
the same place differ. Combine many readings to get a better answer.
"#,
    predict: Some((
        question: "The Rover stands still. Take 40 readings of the distance. How big is their spread (the standard deviation)?",
        unit: "mm",
        measured: "sensor_sigma_mm",
    )),
    criteria: [Report(key: "distance", expected: Measured("sensor_range_m"), tol: 0.005, within_s: Some(5.0))],
    stars: (
        (text: "Within 2 mm", criteria: [Report(key: "distance", expected: Measured("sensor_range_m"), tol: 0.002, within_s: Some(5.0))]),
        (text: "Within 2 mm in under 1 second", criteria: [Report(key: "distance", expected: Measured("sensor_range_m"), tol: 0.002, within_s: Some(1.0))]),
    ),
    hints: (
        "Print 20 readings and look at them. They scatter around the true value.",
        "The average of N noisy readings scatters about sqrt(N) times less than one reading. The median is better when some readings are far off.",
        "The sensor makes a new reading every 50 ms, so axl_step(50) gives you a new one each time. Add up the readings in a loop and divide by how many there are. Ignore readings of -1.",
    ),
)
